*Frameworks like Jackson and Hibernate instantiate via the no-arg constructor, so keep it when they're involved.*

```java
class Pizza {
    String size; boolean cheese;
    Pizza() { this.size = "medium"; this.cheese = false; }            // dup
    Pizza(String size) { this.size = size; this.cheese = false; }     // dup
    Pizza(String size, boolean cheese) { this.size = size; this.cheese = cheese; }
}
// Validation/defaults repeated in every constructor — easy to drift.
```

```java
class Pizza {
    String size; boolean cheese;
    Pizza()                     { this("medium"); }          // delegates
    Pizza(String size)          { this(size, false); }       // delegates
    Pizza(String size, boolean cheese) {                     // primary ctor
        if (size == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("size");
        this.size = size; this.cheese = cheese;              // validation in ONE place
    }
}
```
*`this(...)` must be the **first statement** in the constructor, and all paths funnel into the primary constructor.*

```java
class Rectangle {
    int width, height;

    Rectangle() {              // calls the 2-arg constructor
        this(1, 1);            // must be the FIRST statement
    }
    Rectangle(int side) {      // a square
        this(side, side);      // delegates again
    }
    Rectangle(int width, int height) {   // primary constructor
        if (width <= 0 || height <= 0)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("dimensions must be positive");
        this.width = width; this.height = height; // the only place that assigns
    }
}
new Rectangle();    // -> this(1,1)
new Rectangle(5);   // -> this(5,5)
```

```java
Rectangle() {
    System.out.println("creating"); // COMPILE ERROR
    this(1, 1);                     // this() must be first
}
```

```java
Rectangle() {
    this(1, 1);                     // first statement, OK
    System.out.println("created"); // any extra logic goes AFTER
}
